         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Thinking\n\nConsidering everything we have seen until now, can we reach the conclusion that multiplying by 2 is the same as adding 2 by 2?",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "6fbed3b4-9983-49e0-8863-0295bd54ff3d",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "yes",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "6fbed3b4-9983-49e0-8863-0295bd54ff3d",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "nerd",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "8bbd4fa3-d3cf-4ff4-bd96-65dc852e508b",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=8bbd4fa3-d3cf-4ff4-bd96-65dc852e508b",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Visualizing\n\nTo help you visualize this, let us count objects that are present, 10 times.\n\nSelect one of the objects in the selector below. Then click on the multiplications one by one and verify how many of these objects are shown when each group holds 2 of them.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "0515e27b-7f66-4301-9288-13acde844b52",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "0515e27b-7f66-4301-9288-13acde844b52",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "inlove",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "8bbd4fa3-d3cf-4ff4-bd96-65dc852e508b",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=8bbd4fa3-d3cf-4ff4-bd96-65dc852e508b",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### A Rule\n\nConsidering that we are always advancing 2 by 2, and as a consequence we are always doubling the counter, we can understand this table step by step. For example, if I count the number 2 twice, I have 4.\n\nAs a consequence of this, it is possible to understand this multiplication table as:\n\n**N + N = 2 × N**",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "7b6f4fa1-271d-4261-b7b2-18e9dc5c534f",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "7b6f4fa1-271d-4261-b7b2-18e9dc5c534f",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "nerd",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "130b6adb-161d-4fec-98e4-eaa384122753",
               "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
               "page": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EvenNumber.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2024",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "8bbd4fa3-d3cf-4ff4-bd96-65dc852e508b",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=8bbd4fa3-d3cf-4ff4-bd96-65dc852e508b",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Reading the Rule\n\nLike the multiplication tables before, we can extract important information from this new recipe:\n\n**N + N = 2 × N**\n\nor, written with the multiplication first: **2 × N = N + N**\n\nRead it aloud: \"two times N equals N plus N.\"\n\n- The product is always the **double** of N — the number added to itself.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one rule covers every multiplication by two at once.\n- If **N** is 0, the product is 0; if **N** is 1, the product is 2 — exactly what we saw in the table of zero and the table of one.\n- Every answer of this table is an **even number**; this means its last digit is always 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "4d602ff1-cee2-4cf9-bff5-aee34fe4728c",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "4d602ff1-cee2-4cf9-bff5-aee34fe4728c",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "8bbd4fa3-d3cf-4ff4-bd96-65dc852e508b",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=8bbd4fa3-d3cf-4ff4-bd96-65dc852e508b",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this class, we learned the multiplication table of two. We learned that multiplying any number by 2 is the same as adding it to itself: the result is always the **double** of N.\n\nWe also discovered that every answer of this table is an **even number** — it always ends in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8.\n\nWith the tables of zero, one, and two, our multiplication map keeps growing!",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
